@meta
id: 07-11
title: Top, middle and bottom of the screen
chapter: 07
type: lesson
skills: screen-HML, find-f, line-end
par: 43
minutes: 5
vscode: three clicks, or three careful drags of the cursor
open: order.entity.ts

@brief
Thirteen lines. The whole file is on the screen in front of you, which is
the case that the last few lessons have not covered: no jumping, no
scrolling, everything already visible, and three edits to make.

  1. The first line is missing its `export`. It should read
     `export type Order = {`.

  2. The seventh line, `currency`, is optional on legacy orders:
     `currency?: string;`.

  3. The last line needs one more member on the union, at the end and
     before the semicolon: `| 'REFUNDED'`.

The edits are at the top, the middle and the bottom of what you can see.
That is not a coincidence; it is the lesson.

@keys
| `H` | the highest line on the screen |
| `M` | the middle line on the screen |
| `L` | the lowest line on the screen |
| `I` | insert at the first non-blank |
| `f:` | forward to the colon |
| `$` | end of the line |

@hints
There are three motions that move by where something is on your screen
rather than by where it is in the file. They are the first letters of high,
middle and low.
--
`H`, `M` and `L`. When a file is shorter than the window — as this one is —
they are the first line, the middle line and the last line of the file.
When it is longer, they are the first, middle and last lines you can
currently see, which is a different thing and the whole point of them.
--
`H` `I` `export ` `Esc` does the first. `M` `f:` `i` `?` `Esc` does the
second. `L` `$` `i` puts you just before the final semicolon for the third.

@solution
    H I export  <Esc>
    M f : i ? <Esc>
    L $ i  | 'REFUNDED' <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Thirty-seven keystrokes, and none of them was a line number.

`H`, `M` and `L` are the answer to a question the other motions cannot
answer: "that thing, there, where I am already looking." Your eye has
already found the line. You do not know its number, you do not know how far
down it is, and working either of those out costs more than the jump is
worth.

They take counts, and the counts read oddly at first: `3H` is the third line
from the top of the screen and `3L` is the third from the bottom. So the
full set is a little grid — anywhere in the top few lines, anywhere in the
bottom few, and the middle.

The habit to build is noticing which question you are answering. "Line 74"
is `74G`. "The next block" is `}`. "That line I can see near the bottom" is
`L` or `3L`. Reaching for the wrong family is what makes people feel that
vim is slow: it is not that the motion is missing, it is that they are
asking with the wrong one.

@start order.entity.ts
type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  reference: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  amount: number;
  currency: string;
  placedAt: Date;
  cancelledAt: Date;
  cancellationReason: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'COMPLETED' | 'CANCELLED';

@expected order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  reference: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  amount: number;
  currency?: string;
  placedAt: Date;
  cancelledAt: Date;
  cancellationReason: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'COMPLETED' | 'CANCELLED' | 'REFUNDED';
